hadiths[112]{hadithnumber,text}:
"139901","Yahya related to me from Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Zaynab bint Abi Salama from Umm Salama"
"139902","Yahya related to me from Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Zaynab bint Abi Salama from Umm Salama"
"140001","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that Umar ibn al-Khattab had a dispute brought to him between a muslim and a jew. Umar saw that the right belonged to the jew and decided in his favour. The jew said to him"
"140002","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that Umar ibn al-Khattab had a dispute brought to him between a muslim and a jew. Umar saw that the right belonged to the jew and decided in his favour. The jew said to him"
"140101","Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from his father from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman from Abu Amra al-Ansari from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani that the Messenger of Allah"
"140102","Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from his father from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman from Abu Amra al-Ansari from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani that the Messenger of Allah"
"140201","Yahya related to me from Malik that it reached him that Muhammad ibn Sirin used to say: ""Do not sell grain in its ears until it turns white (ripens)."" Malik said: Whoever buys food at a known price to a named term, then when the term comes due, the one who owes the food says to the owner: ""I have no food, so sell me the food you have on me on credit."" The owner says: ""This is not proper because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade selling food until it is taken possession of."" The debtor says: ""Sell me food on credit until I pay you."" This is not proper because he only gives him food then returns it to him. The gold he gave becomes the price of what was due on him, and the food he gave becomes permissible between them. That would be selling food before taking possession if they did that. Malik said: Regarding a man who has food due from a man (he bought it from him) and his creditor has food due from a man like that food. The debtor says to his creditor: ""I refer you to a debtor of mine who has like the food you have on me, for your food which you have on me."" Malik said: If the food he owes is only food he bought, and he wants to refer his creditor with food he bought, that is not proper. That is selling food before taking possession. But if the food is a salam (advance) due immediately, there is no harm in referring with it because that is not a sale. Selling food before taking possession is not permitted due to the Prophet's ﷺ prohibition, except that the people of knowledge have agreed that there is no harm in partnership (shirkah), tawliyah (selling at cost), and iqalah (rescission) in food and other things. Malik said: That is because the people of knowledge treated it as customary practice (ma'ruf), not as sale. Like a man who advances deficient dirhams (nuqqas) and is paid full weight dirhams (wazinah) with excess. That is permitted for him. But if he bought deficient dirhams for full weight, that would not be permitted even if he stipulated full weight when he advanced it and he only gave deficient. Malik said: Similar to that is that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade muzabana (selling fresh dates on the palm for dried dates by estimation) but granted concession in 'ariyya (selling by estimation). The difference is that muzabana is sale by way of comparison and trade, while 'ariyya is by way of custom, no comparison in it. Malik said: A man should not buy food for a quarter, third, or fraction of a dirham on condition that he be given food on credit. But no harm in buying food for a fraction of a dirham on credit, then giving a dirham and taking with the remainder of his dirham some merchandise, because he gave the fraction due on him in silver and took merchandise with the rest of his dirham. No harm in that. Malik said: No harm in a man placing a dirham with a man, then taking from him for a quarter, third, or known fraction a known merchandise. If there is no known price in that and the man says: ""I take from you at the price of each day,"" that is not permitted because it is gharar (uncertainty), it decreases once and increases once, and they did not separate on a known sale. Malik said: Whoever sells food in bulk (jizaf) without excepting anything, then decides to buy some of it back, it is not proper for him to buy any of it except what he was allowed to except, which is a third or less. If it exceeds a third, it becomes muzabana and what is disliked. He should not buy any of it except what he was allowed to except. He is not allowed to except more than a third. This is the matter about which there is no difference among us."
"140202","Yahya related to me from Malik that he had heard that Muhammad
Sirin used to say, ""Do not sell grain on the ears until it is white.""


Malik said, ""If someone buys food for a known price to be
delivered at a stated date, and when the date comes, the one who owes
the food says, 'I do not have any food, sell me the food which I owe
you with delayed terms.' The owner of the food says, 'This is not
good, because the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant
him peace, forbade selling food until the deal was completed.' The one
who owes the food says to his creditor, 'Sell me any kind of food on
delayed terms until I discharge the debt to you.' This is not good
because he gives him food and then he returns it to him. The gold
which he gave him becomes the price of that which is his right against
him and the food which he gave him becomes what clears what is between
them. If they do that, it becomes the sale of food before the deal is
complete.""


Malik spoke about a man who was owed food which he
had purchased from a man and this man was owed the like of that food
by another man. The one who owed the food said to his creditor, ""I
will refer you to my debtor who owes me the same amount of food as I
owe you, so that you may obtain the food which I owe you .""


Malik said, ""If the man who had to deliver the food, had gone out, and
bought the food to pay off his creditor, that is not good. That is
selling food before taking possession of it. If the food is an advance
which falls due at that particular time, there is no harm in paying
off his creditor with it because that is nota sale. It is not halal to
sell food before receiving it in full since the Messenger of Allah,
may Allah bless him and grant him peace, forbade that. However, the
people of knowledge agree that there is no harm in partnership,
transfer of responsibility and revocation in sales of food and other
goods.""


Malik said, ""That is because the people of knowledge
consider it as a favour rendered. They do not consider it as a sale.
It is like a man lending light dirhams. He is then paid back in
dirhams of full weight, and so gets back more than he lent. That is
halal for him and permitted. Had a man bought defective dirhams from
him as being the full weight, that would not be halal. Had it been
stipulated to him that he lend full weight in dirhams, and then he
gave faulty ones, that would not be halal for him."""
"140301","Yahya related to me from Malik, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Hanzalah."
"140302","Yahya related to me from Malik, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Hanzalah ibn Qays al-Zurayqi, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade the renting of farmland. Hanzalah said: \So I asked Rafi' ibn Khadij"
"140401","Yahya said from Malik that he heard from Sulayman ibn Yasar and others that when they were asked whether the testimony of a man flogged for a hadd crime was permitted"
"140402","Yahya said from Malik that he heard from Sulayman ibn Yasar and others that when they were asked whether the testimony of a man flogged for a hadd crime was permitted"
"140501","And Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Yunus bin Yusuf, from Sa'id bin Al-Musayyab, that Umar bin Al-Khattab passed by Hatib bin Abi Balta'ah while he was selling his raisins in the market. Umar bin Al-Khattab said to him: \Either you increase the price"
"140502","And Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, that he asked Salim bin Abdullah bin Umar about renting out farms. He said: \There is no harm in it for gold and silver.\"" Ibn Shihab said: \""So I said to him: What do you think about the hadith that is mentioned from Rafi' bin Khadij?\"" He said: \""Rafi' exaggerated. If I had a farm"
"140601","Yahya related to me from Malik that it reached him that Uthman ibn Affan used to forbid hoarding (al-hukra)."
"140602","Malik related to me that it reached him that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf rented a piece of land, and it remained in his possession for rent until he died. His son said: ""I only thought it was ours because of how long it had remained in his possession, until he mentioned it to us at his death, and he ordered us to pay off something that was due on him from its rent, in gold or silver."""
"140701","From Malik from Abu'z-Zinad that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab who was the governor of Kufa"
"140702","From Malik from Abu'z-Zinad that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab who was the governor of Kufa"
"140801","Malik related to me that he heard that Abu Salama ibn Abd ar- Rahman and Sulayman ibn Yasar were both asked"
"140802","Malik related to me that he heard that Abu Salama ibn Abd ar- Rahman and Sulayman ibn Yasar were both asked"
"140901","Yahya said that Malik spoke about a man who died and had a debt owing to him and there was one witness"
"140902","Yahya said that Malik spoke about a man who died and had a debt owing to him and there was one witness"
"141001","Yahya related to me from Malik from Nafi' from Abdullah ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the sale of habal al-habala. It was a sale practiced by the people of the Jahiliyya where a man would buy the offspring of a she-camel until she gives birth, then the offspring of that which is in her womb."
"141002","Malik related to me that it reached him from Sulayman ibn Yasar the like of that."
"141101","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab that he said: ""There is no riba in animals. Rather, only three things were forbidden in animals: al-madamin, al-malaqih, and habal al-habala. Al-madamin is the sale of what is in the wombs of female camels. Al-malaqih is the sale of what is on the backs of male camels (i.e., their future offspring)."" Malik said: ""No one should buy a specific animal when it is absent from him, even if he has seen it and approved of it, on condition that he pays its price, whether near or far."" Malik said: ""That was only disliked because the seller benefits from the price, and it is not known whether that merchandise will be found as the buyer saw it or not. For that reason it was disliked. There is no harm in it if it is guaranteed and described."""
"141102","Yahya said: Malik said from Muhammad ibn Umara from Abu Bakr ibn Hazm that Uthman ibn Affan said: ""When boundaries are established in land, there is no right of preemption (shuf'a) in it, nor in a well, nor in a palm pollinator."""
"141201","Malik related to me from al-Ala ibn Abd ar-Rahman from Mabad ibn Kab as-Salami from his brother Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik al-Ansari from Abu Umama that the Messenger of Allah"
"141202","Malik related to me from al-Ala ibn Abd ar-Rahman from Mabad ibn Kab as-Salami from his brother Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik al-Ansari from Abu Umama that the Messenger of Allah"
"141301","Yahya said that Malik had said from Da'ud ibn al-Husayn that he heard Abu Ghatafan ibn Tarif al-Muriyi say"
"141302","Yahya said that Malik had said from Da'ud ibn al-Husayn that he heard Abu Ghatafan ibn Tarif al-Muriyi say"
"141401","Yahya related to me from Malik from Abu al-Zinad from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab that he used to say: ""The sale of a live animal for meat (slaughtered) was forbidden."" Abu al-Zinad said: I said to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab: ""What do you think of a man who buys a she-camel for ten sheep?"" Sa'id said: ""If he bought it to slaughter it, there is no good in that."" Abu al-Zinad said: ""All the people I encountered forbade the sale of a live animal for meat."" Abu al-Zinad said: ""That used to be written in the contracts of the governors in the time of Aban ibn Uthman and Hisham ibn Isma'il, forbidding that."""
"141402","Yahya related to us from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from his father from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman from Abu Amra al-Ansari from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Shall I not inform you of the best of witnesses? The one who brings his testimony before he is asked for it, or informs of his testimony before he is asked for it."""
"141501","Malik related to me from Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman that he said: ""There came to Umar ibn al-Khattab a man from the people of Iraq..."""
"141502","Malik related to me from Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman that he said: ""There came to Umar ibn al-Khattab a man from the people of Iraq who said: 'I have come to you for a matter that has no head nor tail.' Umar said: 'What is it?' He said: 'False testimony has appeared in our land.' Umar said: 'Has that happened?' He said: 'Yes.' Umar said: 'By Allah, no man will be given ease in Islam except through the upright (al-udul).'"""
"141601","And Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr bin Abdur Rahman bin Al-Harith bin Hisham, from Abu Mas'ud Al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) prohibited the price of a dog, the dowry of a prostitute, and the fee of a soothsayer. Meaning by the dowry of a prostitute what a woman is given for fornication, and the fee of a soothsayer is his bribe and what is given for soothsaying."
"141602","And Malik narrated to me that it reached him that Umar bin Al-Khattab said: \The testimony of an opponent or someone suspected is not permissible.\"""""
"141701","Malik related to me from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Qari that his father said"
"141702","Malik related to me from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Qari that his father said"
"141801","Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Sa'id from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad that he said: ""I heard Abdullah ibn Abbas, and a man asked him about a man who gave an advance payment (salam) for slaves (saba'ib) and then wanted to sell them before taking possession of them. Ibn Abbas said: 'That is silver for silver,' and he disliked that."" Malik said: ""And that, in our view - and Allah knows best - is because he wanted to sell them back to the one he bought them from for more than the price he paid in the advance. But if he sold them to someone other than the one he bought them from, there would be no harm in it."" Malik said: ""The agreed-upon practice among us concerning one who gives an advance payment for slaves, livestock, or goods: if each item is described and an advance is made to a fixed term, and the term comes due, the buyer may not sell any of that back to the one he bought it from for more than the advance price before taking possession of what he advanced for. That is because if he does so, it is usury (riba): the buyer, if he gave the seller dinars or dirhams and benefited from them, then when the goods became due and the buyer had not taken possession, he sold them back to the seller for more than the advance price, so it became as if he returned the advance to him and added to it from his own wealth."" Malik said: ""Whoever advances gold or silver for livestock or goods, described, to a named term, then when the term comes due, there is no harm in the buyer selling those goods back to the seller before the term falls due or after it falls due, for other goods (arad) which he takes immediately and does not defer, whatever the amount of those goods, except food. For it is not lawful to sell it until he takes possession of it. And the buyer may sell those goods to someone other than the one he bought them from for gold, silver, or other goods which he takes immediately and does not defer. Because if he defers that, it becomes reprehensible and enters into what is disliked of selling a debt for a debt (kali' bi al-kali'). And selling a debt for a debt is when a man sells a debt he has on a man for a debt on another man."" Malik said: ""And whoever advances for goods to a term, and those goods are of what is not eaten or drunk, the buyer may sell them to whomever he wishes for cash or goods before he receives them in full, provided it is not to the one he bought them from. And he should not sell them to the one he bought them from except for goods which he takes immediately and does not defer."" Malik said: ""And if the goods have not yet fallen due, there is no harm in selling them to the seller for goods different from them, a clear difference, which he takes immediately and does not defer."" Malik said: ""Concerning one who advances dinars or dirhams for four described garments to a term: when the term falls due, the owner demands them but does not find them with him, and finds instead garments inferior to them of the same type. The one who owes the garments says: 'I will give you eight garments of these mine in their place.' There is no harm in that if he takes those garments he is giving before they part. But if a term enters into it, it is not valid. And even if it is before the term falls due, it is not valid either, unless he sells him garments not of the type of the garments he advanced for."""
"141802","Yahya related to me from Malik from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih as- Samman from his father from Abu Hurayra that Sad ibn Ubada said to the Messenger of Allah"
"141901","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that a Syrian man called Ibn Khaybari found a man with his wife and killed him"
"141902","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that a Syrian man called Ibn Khaybari found a man with his wife and killed him"
"142001","Yahya said that Malik related from Ibn Shihab that Sunayn Abi Jamila"
"142002","Yahya said that Malik related from Ibn Shihab that Sunayn Abi Jamila"
"142101","Yahya said from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr that A'isha"
"142102","Yahya said from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr that A'isha"
"142201","Malik related to me from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hadi from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith at-Taymi from Sulayman ibn Yasar from Abdullah ibn Abi Umayya that a woman's husband died"
"142202","Malik related to me from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hadi from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith at-Taymi from Sulayman ibn Yasar from Abdullah ibn Abi Umayya that a woman's husband died"
"142301","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Sulayman ibn Yasar that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to attach the children of the Jahiliyya to whoever claimed them in Islam. Two men came and each of them claimed a woman's child. Umar ibn al-Khattab summoned a person who scrutinized features and he looked at them. The scrutinizer said"
"142302","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Sulayman ibn Yasar that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to attach the children of the Jahiliyya to whoever claimed them in Islam. Two men came and each of them claimed a woman's child. Umar ibn al-Khattab summoned a person who scrutinized features and he looked at them. The scrutinizer said"
"142401","Malik related to me that he had heard that Umar ibn al-Khattab or Uthman ibn Affan gave a judgement about a slave woman who misled a man about herself and said that she was free. He married her and she bore children. It was decided that he should ransom his children with their like of slaves. Yahya said that he heard Malik say"
"142402","Malik related to me that he had heard that Umar ibn al-Khattab or Uthman ibn Affan gave a judgement about a slave woman who misled a man about herself and said that she was free. He married her and she bore children. It was decided that he should ransom his children with their like of slaves. Yahya said that he heard Malik say"
"142501","Malik narrated to me, from Al-Ala' ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ma'bad ibn Ka'b al-Silmi..."
"142502","Malik narrated to me, from Al-Ala' ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ma'bad ibn Ka'b al-Silmi, from his brother, Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik al-Ansari, from Abu Umamah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Whoever seizes the right of a Muslim by his oath"
"142601","Malik related to me from Nafi that Safiyya bint Abi Ubayd informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab said"
"142602","Malik related to me from Nafi that Safiyya bint Abi Ubayd informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab said"
"142701","Yahya related from Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father that the Messenger of Allah"
"142702","Yahya related from Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father that the Messenger of Allah"
"142801","Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Salim ibn Abdullah from his father that Umar ibn al-Khattab said"
"142802","Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Salim ibn Abdullah from his father that Umar ibn al-Khattab said"
"142901","Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm that he heard that the Messenger of Allah"
"142902","Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm that he heard that the Messenger of Allah"
"143001","Malik related to me from Abu'z-Zinad from al-Araj from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah"
"143002","Malik related to me from Abu'z-Zinad from al-Araj from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah"
"143101","Malik related to me from Abu'r-Rijal Muhammad ibn Abd ar-Rahman from his mother Amra bint Abd ar-Rahman that she informed him that the Messenger of Allah"
"143102","Malik related to me from Abu'r-Rijal Muhammad ibn Abd ar-Rahman from his mother Amra bint Abd ar-Rahman that she informed him that the Messenger of Allah"
"143201","Yahya related to me from Malik from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini from his father that the Messenger of Allah"
"143202","Yahya related to me from Malik from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini from his father that the Messenger of Allah"
"143301","And Malik related to me from Musa ibn Maysara that he heard a man ask Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab: ""I am a man who sells on credit (bi al-dayn)."" Sa'id said: ""Do not sell except what you have brought to your dwelling."" Malik said: ""Concerning the one who buys goods from a man on condition that he delivers those goods to a named term - either for a market he hopes will be favorable, or for a need in that time he stipulated - then the seller fails to deliver at that term, and the buyer wants to return those goods to the seller: that is not for the buyer, and the sale is binding on him. And if the seller brings those goods before the term falls due, the buyer is not compelled to take them."" Malik said: ""Concerning the one who buys food, measures it, then someone comes to buy it from him and informs the comer that he has measured it for himself and taken full possession. The buyer wants to believe him and take it by his measure: whatever is sold on this description for cash, there is no harm in it. But whatever is sold on this description to a term, it is disliked until the other buyer measures it for himself. And the term sale is disliked only because it is a means to usury and a fear that it might be conducted on this basis without measure or weight. If it is to a term, it is disliked, and there is no difference of opinion on this among us."" Malik said: ""One should not buy a debt on an absent man, nor a present one, except with an acknowledgment from the one on whom the debt is, nor on a dead man, even if the estate of the dead man is known. That is because buying that is uncertainty (gharar); it is not known whether it will be fulfilled or not."" He said: ""The explanation of what is disliked of that is that if one buys a debt on an absent or dead man, it is not known what debts may attach to the dead man that were not known. If a debt attaches to the dead man, the price the buyer paid goes to waste."" Malik said: ""And in that there is another defect: he bought something not guaranteed to him, and if it is not fulfilled, his price goes to waste. So this is gharar which is not valid."" Malik said: ""The distinction between 'a man should not sell except what he has' and 'a man may advance for something not in his possession' is that the practitioner of 'ina (a usurious loan disguised as a sale) only brings the gold he wants to buy with and says: 'These are ten dinars; what do you want me to buy for you with them?' As if he sells ten dinars cash for fifteen dinars to a term. Therefore this was disliked. And that is the deception and the fraud."""
"143302","Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from al-Araj from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah"
"143401","Malik related to me from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini from his father that ad-Dahhak ibn Khalifa watered his irrigation ditch from a large source of water. He wanted to have it pass through the land of Muhammad ibn Maslama"
"143402","Malik related to me from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini from his father that ad-Dahhak ibn Khalifa watered his irrigation ditch from a large source of water. He wanted to have it pass through the land of Muhammad ibn Maslama"
"143501","Malik related to me from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini that his father said"
"143502","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Abu Bakr ibn
Abd ar-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham that the Messenger of Allah,
may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, ""Whenever a man sells
wares and then the buyer becomes bankrupt and the seller has not taken
any of the price and he finds some of his property intact with the
buyer, he is more entitled to it than anyone else. If the buyer dies,
then the seller is the same as other creditors with respect to it."""
"143601","Yahya related to me from Malik that Thawr ibn Zayd ad-Dili said"
"143602","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Abu Bakr ibn
Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from Umar ibn Abdal-Aziz from Abu Bakr ibn
Abd ar-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham from Abu Hurayra that the
Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, ""If
anyone goes bankrupt, and a man finds his own property intact with
him, he is more entitled to it than anyone else.""


Malik spoke
about a man who sold a man wares, and the buyer went bankrupt. He
said, ""The seller takes whatever of his goods he finds. If the buyer
has sold some of them and distributed them, the seller of the wares is
more entitled to them than the creditors. What the buyer has
distributed does not prevent the seller from taking whatever of it he
finds. It is the seller's right if he has received any of the price
from the buyer and he wants to return it to take what he finds of his
wares, and in what he does not find, he is like the creditors.""


Malik spoke about some one who bought spun wool or a plot of land,
and then did some work on it, like building a house on the plot of
land or weaving the spun wool into cloth. Then he went bankrupt after
he had bought it, and the original owner of the plot said, ""I will
take the plot and whatever structure is on it."" Malik said, ""That
structure is not his. However, the plot and what is in it that the
buyer has improved is appraised. Then one sees what the price of the
plot is and how much of that value is the price of the structure. They
are partners in that. The owner of the plot has as much as his
portion, and the creditors have the amount of the portion of the
structure.""


Malik said, ""The explanation of that is that the
value of it all is fifteen hundred dirhams. The value of the plot is
five hundred dirhams, and the value of the building is one thousand
dirhams. The owner of the plot has a third, and the creditors have
two-thirds.""


Malik said, ""It is like that with spinning and
other things of the same nature in these circumstances and the buyer
has a debt which he cannot pay. This is the behaviour in such cases.""


Malik said, ""As for goods which have been sold and which the
buyer does not improve, but those goods sell well and have gone up in
price, so their owner wants them and the creditors also want to seize
them, then the creditors choose between giving the owner of the goods
the price for which he sold them and not giving him any loss and
surrendering his goods to him.


""If the price of the goods has
gone down, the one who sold them has a choice. If he likes, he can
take his goods and he has no claim to any of his debtor's property,
and that is his right. If he likes, he can be one of the creditors and
take a portion of his due and not take his goods. That is up to him.""


Malik said about someone who bought a slave-girl or animal
and she gave birth in his possession and the buyer went bankrupt, ""The
slave-girl or the animal and the offspring belong to the seller unless
the creditors desire it. In that case they give him his complete due
and they take it."""
"143701","Yahya said that he heard Malik speak about a man who died and left properties in Aliya and Safila (outlying districts of Madina). He said"
"143702","Yahya said that he heard Malik speak about a man who died and left properties in Aliya and Safila (outlying districts of Madina). He said"
"143801","Malik related to me from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Yahya ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Hatib that some slaves of Hatib stole a she-camel belonging to a man from the Muzayna tribe and they slaughtered it. The case was brought before Umar ibn al-Khattab"
"143802","Malik related to me from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Yahya ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Hatib that some slaves of Hatib stole a she-camel belonging to a man from the Muzayna tribe and they slaughtered it. The case was brought before Umar ibn al-Khattab"
"143901","Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, that it reached him that Umar ibn al-Khattab said regarding a man who advanced food to another man on the condition that he would deliver it in another city, that Umar ibn al-Khattab disliked this and said: \Then where is the carrying?\"" meaning its carriage."""
"143902","Malik narrated to me, from Nafi', from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, that she informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: \What is the matter with men who have intercourse with their slave women and then let them go out? No slave woman comes to me whose master admits to having had intercourse with her"
"144001","A free loan which you lend by which you desire the pleasure of
Allah, and so you have the pleasure of Allah. A free loan which you
lend by which you desire the pleasure of your companion, so you have
the pleasure of your companion, and a free loan which you lend by
which you take what is impure by what is pure, and that is usury."" He
said, ""What do you order me to do, Abu Abd ar-Rahman?"" He said, ""I
think that you should tear up the agreement. If he gives you the like
of what you lent him, accept it. If he gives you less than what you
lent him, take it and you will be rewarded. If he gives you better
than what you lent him, of his own good will, that is his gratitude to
you and you have the wage of the period you gave him the loan."""
"144002","Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr that A'isha"
"144101","Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari that Umar ibn al-Khattab said"
"144102","Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari that Umar ibn al-Khattab said"
"144201","And Malik related to me that it reached him that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud used to say: ""Whoever gives an advance (aslaf salafan) should not stipulate better than it, even if it is a handful of fodder, for that is usury."" Malik said: ""The agreed-upon practice among us is that whoever borrows something of livestock with a known description and quality, there is no harm in that, and he must return its like, except for female slaves (wala'id). For in that there is feared the means to making lawful what is not lawful, so it is not valid. The explanation of what is disliked of that is that a man borrows a slave girl, has his way with her, then returns her to her owner as she is. That is not valid nor lawful, and the people of knowledge have never ceased to forbid it and not permit it for anyone."""
"144202","Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning the floodwaters of Mahzoor and Muzaynab: \It is held back until the ankles"
"144301","Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Nafi, from Abdullah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Let not one of you sell upon the sale of another.\"""""
"144302","Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Excess water shall not be withheld to prevent fodder.\"""""
"144401","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said that Abd ar-Rahman ibn al-Qasim ibn Muhammad heard Makhul ad-Dimashqi ask al-Qasim ibn Muhammad about the life pension and what people said about it. Al- Qasim ibn Muhammad said"
"144402","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said that Abd ar-Rahman ibn al-Qasim ibn Muhammad heard Makhul ad-Dimashqi ask al-Qasim ibn Muhammad about the life pension and what people said about it. Al- Qasim ibn Muhammad said"
"144501","Malik related to me from Nafi that Abdullah ibn Umar inherited the house of Hafsa bint Umar. He said"
"144502","Malik related to me from Nafi that Abdullah ibn Umar inherited the house of Hafsa bint Umar. He said"
"144601","Malik related to me from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman from Yazid"
"144602","Malik related to me from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman from Yazid"
"144701","Malik related to me from Ayyub ibn Musa from Muawiya ibn Abdullah ibn Badr al-Juhani that his father informed him that he stopped with a people on the way to Syria and he found a purse which had eighty dinars in it. He mentioned that to Umar ibn al-Khattab. Umar said to him"
"144702","Malik related to me from Ayyub ibn Musa from Muawiya ibn Abdullah ibn Badr al-Juhani that his father informed him that he stopped with a people on the way to Syria and he found a purse which had eighty dinars in it. He mentioned that to Umar ibn al-Khattab. Umar said to him"
"144801","Malik related to me from Nafi that a man found something and went to Abdullah ibn Umar and said to him"
"144802","Malik related to me from Nafi that a man found something and went to Abdullah ibn Umar and said to him"
"144901","Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, that he asked him about a man who rents a beast of burden and then rents it out for more than he rented it for. He said: \There is no harm in that.\"""""
"144902","Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Thurayh bin Zayd al-Dili, that he said: It has reached me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Any house or land that was divided in the Jahiliyyah"
"145001","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that Umar ibn al-Khattab said while he was leaning his back against the Kaba"
"145002","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that Umar ibn al-Khattab said while he was leaning his back against the Kaba"
"145101","Malik narrated to me, from al-Ala' bin Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from his grandfather, that Uthman bin Affan gave him wealth as a qirad (a type of partnership) to work with, on the condition that the profit would be between them."
"145102","Malik narrated to me, from Hisham bin Urwah, from his father, from Yahya bin Abd al-Rahman bin Hatib, that some slaves of Hatib stole a she-camel belonging to a man from Muzaynah. They slaughtered it. This was brought to Umar bin al-Khattab, and Umar ordered Kathir bin al-Salt to cut off their hands. Then Umar said: \I think you are starving them.\"" Then Umar said: \""By Allah"
"145201","Malik related to me from Said ibn Amr Shurahbil ibn Said ibn Sad ibn Ubada from his father that his father said"
"145202","Malik related to me from Said ibn Amr Shurahbil ibn Said ibn Sad ibn Ubada from his father that his father said"
"145301","Malik related to me from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from A'isha"
"145302","Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr from Aisha, the wife of the Prophet, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, that she said: ""Abu Bakr as-Siddiq had given her twenty wasqs of his property from al-Ghaba as a gift. When his death approached, he said, 'By Allah, my daughter, there is no one among the people after me I would prefer to see wealthy than you, and no one I would be more distressed to see poor. I gave you twenty wasqs as a gift. If you had harvested and taken possession of it, it would have been yours. Today, however, it is the property of the heirs. Your two brothers and your two sisters are the heirs. So divide it according to the Book of Allah"
"145401","Malik related to me that he heard that a man of the Ansar from the tribe of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj"
"145402","Malik related to me that he heard that a man of the Ansar from the tribe of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj"
